n = 6
  |G_n|=56, #SC=12, V_merged=34
  (1) distinct skew-spectra = 6;  distinct U-spectra = 6  (equal => Cayley is a spectral bijection, U carries no more)
  (2) resolution ceiling V_merged=34: distinct-spectra 6 < V_merged (LOSES 28 MORE beyond complement-pairs)
      cospectral collisions = |G_n|-distinct = 50  (>= #NS-pairs = 22 forced by complement-reflection)
  (3) 2nd moment trace(S^2): values=[-30.0]  (constant = -n(n-1) = -30 => BLIND)
      4th moment trace(S^4): #distinct values = 5  (first informative even moment)
      circular trace(U)=sum cos(theta): #distinct values = 6  (Cayley wrap makes the blind 2nd moment informative; note it matches/beats trace(S^4))
  (4) COMBINATORIAL: #distinct H(Redei) = 19, #distinct (H,c3) = 31  (vs #distinct spectra = 6) -- combinatorial counts resolve FAR more than any spectrum
      H all odd (Redei) = True; metagraph 2nd moment Var(H) = 157.551  (THM-589 W(n); the SECOND MOMENT that IS relevant to covering/flip-rank)

==============================================================================
NET: U-spectrum = S-spectrum (Cayley bijection) -> NO eigenvalue-level gain. Both factor
through the MERGED metagraph (ceiling V_merged) because complement is a reflection and the
spectrum is reflection-invariant. The flip-rank excess is carried by the SC (fixed) classes
on the UNMERGED G_n -> SPECTRALLY INVISIBLE. The 2nd moment trace(S^2)=-n(n-1) is the constant
order-2 shadow of that symmetry; Cayley wraps it into an informative circular moment trace(U),
but resolution is still capped at V_merged. To see the excess needs NON-spectral symmetry data.
==============================================================================
